      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Tissue coverage and strut apposition of drug eluting stents (DES), which can be assessed with optical coherence tomography (OCT), may be associated with late stent thrombosis (LST)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Prospective observational angiographic and OCT follow-up at 6 months post-implantation of a biolimus-A9 eluting stent (BES) vs. a paclitaxel eluting stent (PES), with biodegradable polymer carriers. The primary outcome was the percent difference of uncovered struts between BESs and PESs."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "A maximum likelihood model was used for analysis, to account for data clustering. Sixteen patients were treated with BES (28 lesions/4530 struts) and 16 with PES (23 lesions/4450 struts). Overall, BESs compared to PESs had more uncovered [0.41% vs. 0.21%, difference estimate (DE) 0.2 (95% CI, 0.06-0.34), p=0.006], malapposed [0.18% vs. 0.04%, DE 0.14 (95% CI 0.05-0.23), p=0.003], uncovered and malapposed [0.08% vs. 0.026%, DE 0.057 (95% CI 0.015-0.1), p=0.01] and protruding struts [0.23% vs. 0.04%, DE 0.185 (95% CI 0.1-0.27), p<0.001], and significantly lower neointimal thickness (59.3 ± 28.2 μm vs. 201.7 ± 97.5, p<0.001). None of the BESs was totally covered with neointima, in contrast to 5 (21.7%) PESs (p=0.01). Thrombus was detected in 1 (3.6%) BES and 5 (21.7%) PESs (p=0.05); however, no patient experienced clinical stent thrombosis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Between two stents with biodegradable polymer, OCT demonstrated that BESs had more uncovered and malapposed struts compared to PESs at 6 months. This difference might be partly attributed to the more potent antiproliferative properties of biolimus-A9; however, its impact on clinical outcome and on the risk of LST is yet to be determined."
          }
        ]
      },
